our next speaker is eric weingarten now  eric is a postdoc at stanford and is  currently a research fellow in the pro  high dimensional probability program  eric  okay thanks prasad um so in this short  talk  i want to tell you guys about an  algorithms problem  which is taking place in some high  dimensional metric space so the  algorithms problem is the approximate  nearest neighbor problem  and i have this picture here to explain  what the problem is  basically we receive a data set and  think about this as just  n points in some high dimensional space  and what we want to do is we want to  pre-process it into a data structure  such that we can do  approximate similarity search so what  this means is that  after you pre-process your points  there's going to be a query which comes  in this is a blue point  in the center labeled q and the promise  is that there exists some point from the  data set  within distance r from the query so r is  some parameter and  x here is the point from the data set  which is closest to q  and now the task is to return any point  from the data set  within distance c times r from the query  so in the picture here this is the point  x  hat so distance c times r so if c is  bigger than one then you're returning an  approximate nearest neighbor  to the query point q and this problem  sort of shows up and it's really  parameterized by how we measure  distances  in the space so let me tell you about  maybe  one of the most well-known result in  this area which is  when the underlying metric space is the  l1 distance so this is in the next slide  this is a theorem of indica matuani from  1998 and it really shows like the goal  of what we really  want to from these data structures in  particular when p  equals one so this is the metric space  l1 where you take the sum of absolute  values  uh we can basically design a data  structure where the approximation factor  c is one over epsilon  the space is slightly super linear  basically d times  n to the one plus epsilon and the query  time is highly sublinear so we're trying  not to scan through the entire data set  think of epsilon as being like one half  this is time complexity square root n  okay so go to the next slide  on the other hand when we think of p as  being something large like the l  infinity distance suddenly the best  known algorithms that we have  running in sublinear time have to pay a  large factor  in the approximation in particular for p  equals infinity the best algorithm that  we know  that runs in sublinear time has  complexity which is  log log d  so this is much worse than the constant  approximation we got from  when p equals one okay so the next slide  okay so this is basically a picture of  the state of the art and where i  explained the  sort of the new result so we have in the  the y-axis here represents the  approximation factor  and p is represents the the x-axis so we  go from p equals 1  on the lower left getting the constant  approximation to  p equals infinity um  all the way on the right and this  basically this curve  that is supposed to be sort of like log  d is supposed to indicate the  conjectured optimal  algorithm and the new result is that we  can actually match this conjectured  optimal whenever p is bigger than log  log d  this is like some technical reasons this  is the new result with  alexandoni sasho nikolov and elia  rajenstein  and at a high level you can think about  this as kind of interpolating between  the case for p equals one and p equals  infinity which even though  algorithmically look very different  there is a way to smoothly trade these  off and then i  am highlighting this green region here  because this is the open problem  this is still this um this area where we  don't quite have  what the conjectured optimal algorithm  is  yeah so that's the that's everything
 

